<figcaption class="ltx_caption ltx_centering" style="font-size:90%;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_table">Table 1. </span>Test Execution Performance Metrics for Various Techniques and Models</figcaption>
</figure>
<figure id="S4.F3" class="ltx_figure"><img src="2504.20896v1/figures/jointimage.png" id="S4.F3.g1" class="ltx_graphics ltx_centering ltx_img_landscape" style="aspect-ratio:215/104;" width="215" height="104" alt="Refer to caption">
<figcaption class="ltx_caption ltx_centering"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_figure"><span id="S4.F3.3" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:90%;">Figure 3</span>. </span><span id="S4.F3.4" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:90%;">The left histogram shows test case length distribution, with most cases in the 3–10 step range. The right histogram shows that shorter test cases succeed more often, while longer ones have lower success due to increased interactions and error risks</span></figcaption>
</figure>
</section>
<section id="S4.SS0.SSS0.Px3" class="ltx_paragraph">
<h4 class="ltx_title ltx_title_paragraph">Impact of Test Case Length on Execution Success</h4>

<div id="S4.SS0.SSS0.Px3.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.SS0.SSS0.Px3.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Minimum number of test steps needed to complete a test case can affects execution success rate. Figure <a href="#S4.F3" title="Figure 3 ‣ Performance of LELANTE in test case execution. ‣ 4. Evaluation ‣ LELANTE: LEveraging LLM for Automated ANdroid TEsting" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">3</span></a> shows that most test cases have 3–10 steps (left) and that shorter test cases (fewer than 6 steps) succeed more often (right). Longer test cases see lower success due to increased interactions, error risks, and incorrect action selection. This highlights the need for refining test descriptions and improving error recovery.</p>
</div>
<figure id="S4.T2" class="ltx_table">
<table id="S4.T2.3" class="ltx_tabular ltx_centering ltx_align_middle">
<tr id="S4.T2.3.1" class="ltx_tr">
<td id="S4.T2.3.1.1" class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_border_l ltx_border_r ltx_border_t"><span id="S4.T2.3.1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Condition</span></td>
<td id="S4.T2.3.1.2" class="ltx_td ltx_align_center ltx_border_r ltx_border_t"><span id="S4.T2.3.1.2.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Execution Success Rate</span></td></tr>
<tr id="S4.T2.3.2" class="ltx_tr">
<td id="S4.T2.3.2.1" class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_border_l ltx_border_r ltx_border_t">Original Test Cases</td>
<td id="S4.T2.3.2.2" class="ltx_td ltx_align_center ltx_border_r ltx_border_t">65%</td></tr>
<tr id="S4.T2.3.3" class="ltx_tr">
<td id="S4.T2.3.3.1" class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_border_b ltx_border_l ltx_border_r">Modified Test Cases</td>
<td id="S4.T2.3.3.2" class="ltx_td ltx_align_center ltx_border_b ltx_border_r">73%</td></tr>
</table>
<figcaption class="ltx_caption ltx_centering"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_table"><span id="S4.T2.4" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:90%;">Table 2</span>. </span><span id="S4.T2.5" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:90%;">Impact of Test Case Modifications (Ambiguity Resolution of Test Case Descriptions) on Execution Success Rate</span></figcaption>
</figure>
</section>
<section id="S4.SS0.SSS0.Px4" class="ltx_paragraph">
<h4 class="ltx_title ltx_title_paragraph">Impact of Ambiguity Resolution in Test Cases</h4>

<div id="S4.SS0.SSS0.Px4.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.SS0.SSS0.Px4.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Ambiguous test descriptions reduced execution reliability. For example, <span id="S4.SS0.SSS0.Px4.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">"Change notification settings"</span> lacked details on which settings to modify, leading <span id="S4.SS0.SSS0.Px4.p1.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_slanted">LELANTE</span> to misinterpret steps. Refining descriptions (e.g., <span id="S4.SS0.SSS0.Px4.p1.1.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">"Disable email notifications from the Settings menu"</span>) improved success rates from 65% to 73% (Table <a href="#S4.T2" title="Table 2 ‣ Impact of Test Case Length on Execution Success ‣ 4. Evaluation ‣ LELANTE: LEveraging LLM for Automated ANdroid TEsting" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">2</span></a>). Guidelines for resolving ambiguity are in Appendix <a href="#A1" title="Appendix A Guidelines for Formatting Test Cases ‣ LELANTE: LEveraging LLM for Automated ANdroid TEsting" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">A</span></a>.</p>
</div>
</section>
</section>
<section id="S5" class="ltx_section">
<h2 class="ltx_title ltx_title_section"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_section">5. </span>Related Works</h2>

<div id="S5.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S5.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Automated mobile application testing has traditionally relied on script-based frameworks such as <span id="S5.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Appium</span> and <span id="S5.p1.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Espresso</span> <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib27" title="" class="ltx_ref">Team, 2025</a>; <a href="#bib.bib4" title="" class="ltx_ref">Developers, 2025</a>)</cite>, which allow developers to write scripts that simulate user interactions. While these tools provide consistency in test execution, they require extensive manual effort to maintain scripts as applications evolve, making them inefficient for large-scale automation. To mitigate script maintenance overhead, record-and-replay mechanisms, such as <span id="S5.p1.1.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">RERAN</span> <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib16" title="" class="ltx_ref">Lam et al., 2017</a>)</cite>, capture user interactions for deterministic execution. However, these approaches remain fragile when handling dynamic UI changes requiring manual adaptation of the scripts and limiting their effectiveness in real-world scenarios. In contrast, LELANTE’s LLM driven execution directly interprets human-language test cases without requiring any pre-written scripts.</p>
</div>
<div id="S5.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S5.p2.1" class="ltx_p">To improve scalability, search-based techniques such as <span id="S5.p2.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Stoat</span>  <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib26" title="" class="ltx_ref">Su et al., 2017</a>)</cite> and <span id="S5.p2.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Sapienz</span>  <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib21" title="" class="ltx_ref">Mao et al., 2016</a>)</cite> employ probabilistic and evolutionary algorithms for automated exploratory testing. Although effective in broadening test coverage, these methods often require substantial computational resources and manual validation of results. Recently, AI-driven methods such as <span id="S5.p2.1.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">AutoDroid</span> <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib30" title="" class="ltx_ref">Wen et al., 2024</a>)</cite> leverage LLMs to automate single-step UI interactions. However, AutoDroid primarily focuses on task execution without explicitly handling incorrect execution paths or error recovery. In contrast, <span id="S5.p2.1.4" class="ltx_text ltx_font_slanted">LELANTE</span> adopts an iterative, reasoning-driven approach specifically designed for testers, emphasizing robustness through dynamic error detection and recovery.</p>
</div>
<div id="S5.p3" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S5.p3.1" class="ltx_p">Multimodal frameworks integrating vision-language models <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib20" title="" class="ltx_ref">Liu et al., 2024a</a>; <a href="#bib.bib31" title="" class="ltx_ref">Wu et al., 2024</a>)</cite> have also been explored for UI understanding and verification. However, these models typically involve high computational costs due to heavy reliance on visual processing, limiting practical deployment at scale. <span id="S5.p3.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_slanted">LELANTE</span>, on the other hand, relies solely on textual representation derived from a GUI refinement process (§ <a href="#S3.SS1" title="3.1. Screen Representation Refinement ‣ 3. Methodology ‣ LELANTE: LEveraging LLM for Automated ANdroid TEsting" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">3.1</span></a>), which enhances the effectiveness of prompt engineering, improves model performance, and reduces deployment costs.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S6" class="ltx_section">
<h2 class="ltx_title ltx_title_section"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_section">6. </span>Discussion</h2>

<div id="S6.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S6.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S6.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_slanted">LELANTE</span> demonstrates a significant step in automating Android testing, achieving a 73% success rate and 78% error recovery. However, performance declines with longer test cases, highlighting challenges in handling ambiguity and error propagation. While GUI refinement improves execution, reliance on manual test case modifications suggests limitations in natural language understanding. Another limitation of <span id="S6.p1.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_slanted">LELANTE</span> is its inability to autonomously verify if an executed test case successfully achieves its expected results. Consequently, human validation remains essential to confirm whether the expected final application state is correctly attained. Additionally, the current backtracking mechanism remains relatively slow during error recovery, necessitating improvements on reasoning mechanism and disambiguation strategy.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S7" class="ltx_section">
<h2 class="ltx_title ltx_title_section"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_section">7. </span>Conclusion and Future Work</h2>

<div id="S7.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S7.p1.1" class="ltx_p">In this work, we propose <span id="S7.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_slanted">LELANTE</span> , an LLM-driven framework for automating the execution of test cases for Android application. <span id="S7.p1.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_slanted">LELANTE</span> utilizes a refined screen representation and a structured prompt to guide an LLM in generating execution plans for test cases.  <span id="S7.p1.1.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_slanted">LELANTE</span>’s reasoning-driven action selection and automated backtracking for error correction results in an enhanced execution efficiency and adaptability to dynamic UI changes. Our work paves the way for further advancements in AI-driven test automation, reducing developer effort, improving software quality and eliminating the need for manual script writing for test case automation. Future efforts will focus on automating test outcome (i.e., expected results) validation to reduce human dependency, optimizing backtracking performance, and investigating prompt-based or incremental learning techniques for leveraging historical execution data without extensive model fine-tuning.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S8" class="ltx_section">
<h2 class="ltx_title ltx_title_section"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_section">8. </span>Acknowledgments</h2>

<div id="S8.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S8.p1.1" class="ltx_p">This work was supported by Samsung R&amp;D Institute Bangladesh (SRBD) and Bangladesh University of Engineering and Technology (BUET).</p>
</div>
</section>
